wsAbout":["Insurance appraisal","Appraisal clause","Hurricane claims","Wind and flood causation","Xactimate estimating","Umpire services"],"openingHoursSpecification":[{"@type":"OpeningHoursSpecification","dayOfWeek":["Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday"],"opens":"08:00","closes":"18:00"}]},{"@type":"WebSite","@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/#website","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/","name":"South Carolina Insurance Appraiser","publisher":{"@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"inLanguage":"en-US"},{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#webpage","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/","name":"Insurance Appraisal Process in South Carolina, Step by Step","description":"The insurance appraisal process in South Carolina: written demand, appointing appraisers, choosing an umpire, negotiation and the binding award.","inLanguage":"en-US","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/#website"},"about":{"@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"dateModified":"2026-11-12","primaryImageOfPage":{"@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#primaryimage"},"breadcrumb":{"@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#breadcrumb"},"mainEntity":{"@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#howto"}},{"@type":"ImageObject","@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#primaryimage","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp","width":1200,"height":800,"caption":"Notebook, pen and coffee cup on a table during a meeting"},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Appraisal process","item":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/"}],"@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#breadcrumb"},{"@type":"HowTo","name":"The insurance appraisal process in South Carolina","description":"How a disputed property claim moves through the appraisal clause, from the written demand to a signed award.","step":[{"@type":"HowToStep","position":1,"name":"Spot the gap and try a supplement","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#step-1","text":"Appraisal starts after the insurer has accepted at least part of the claim and issued an estimate you believe is too low. Before anyone demands anything, compare that estimate line by line with your contractor’s number. Look for missing rooms, roof squares counted short, absent code items, no…"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":2,"name":"Send a written appraisal demand","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#step-2","text":"Either side may invoke appraisal, and the demand should always be in writing. A clear demand identifies the policy and claim numbers, states that the parties disagree on the amount of loss, cites the appraisal provision, and names your appraiser or says you will do so within the policy’s time…"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":3,"name":"Each side appoints an appraiser","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#step-3","text":"You and the insurer each choose a competent and disinterested appraiser, commonly within 20 days of the demand. “Disinterested” means your appraiser should have no financial stake in the outcome beyond a fair fee and should not be the contractor hoping to do the repair. Your appraiser reviews…"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":4,"name":"Agree on an umpire","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#step-4","text":"The two appraisers then pick a neutral umpire, often within 15 days. Good practice is to settle on the umpire early, before positions harden, so that person is ready if the appraisers stall on a few items. If the appraisers can’t agree on an umpire, most policies let either party ask a court of…"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":5,"name":"Inspect the property, exchange estimates","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#step-5","text":"Both appraisers inspect the damage, ideally together so they can agree on measurements and conditions on the spot. Expect roof slopes, pitches and squares to be measured, interiors to be checked with moisture meters, and attics to be examined for hidden framing damage after a wind or tree loss.…"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":6,"name":"Negotiate line by line","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#step-6","text":"The appraisers work through the estimates item by item. Most lines settle quickly once both sides see the same photos and measurements. The remaining disputes tend to cluster around a familiar list: Matching of shingles, siding or flooring that can’t be blended. Ordinance or law items required…"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":7,"name":"The umpire decides open items","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#step-7","text":"Any items the appraisers can’t resolve go to the umpire. Each appraiser submits a short position on each disputed line with supporting photos, pricing and code references. Some umpires inspect the property themselves; others decide from the record. The umpire picks a value for each open item,…"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":8,"name":"Award, payment and depreciation","url":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#step-8","text":"The signed award states the amount of loss, often split into replacement cost and actual cash value. The insurer then applies your deductible and prior payments, and issues payment within the time the policy allows. If your policy pays replacement cost, the carrier may hold back recoverable…"}],"@id":"https://southcarolinainsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-explained/#howto"}]}</script>
